    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`python -m app.server --http` now works: `serve_http(host, port)` in `app/server.py` serves the inventory server over MCP Streamable HTTP at `/mcp` and blocks until the process is stopped.\n\nIt's one line: `build_server().run(\"streamable-http\", host=host, port=port, streamable_http_path=\"/mcp\")`. In the installed `mcp` 2.2.0, host and port are passed to `run()` rather than to the server's constructor. No dependencies were added, and stdio mode is unchanged.\n\n**Tests:** `pytest -q` passes (2 tests). I also started the server on port 8765 and connected with the MCP Streamable HTTP client. The handshake worked, `list_tools()` returned `stock` and `reserve`, and the server kept running until I stopped it.\n\n**One thing for you:** the temporary test script `_smoke.py` is still in the project root. My command to delete it needed your approval and wasn't run, so please delete it yourself.\n\nRemote agents can only reach the server if you start it with `--host 0.0.0.0` (or your machine's address), because the default `127.0.0.1` accepts local connections only. For local hosts like that, the MCP library turns on DNS-rebinding protection automatically. On any other host it's off unless you set `transport_security`, and there's no authentication. If the server will be reachable beyond a trusted network, you'll probably want both."
  },
